From 2c286740ae1faf17ff6a9801c1bb5aa35d4f9135 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: duncan0k <dev@ark0.si>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 22:45:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] consensus: close the P2MR rescue lane again from a height,
 the kill switch

The kill switch of the M4-R design (section 11), prepared and tested
with M4-R1 and not released with it: if the proof system turns out to
allow forgeries or malleated proofs, or the verifier a bug that passes
an invalid proof, a build with this patch closes the rescue lane from a
height on. It only restricts the rules, a soft fork, and needs no key.

- A buried deployment p2mr_rescue_end. From its height GetBlockScriptFlags
  sets SCRIPT_VERIFY_P2MR_NO_RESCUE again, so that a rescue token is a
  signature like any other. ARK0_RESCUE_END_HEIGHT is left unset, to be
  chosen when the patch is used; another custom signet gives it with
  -signetrescueend, above -signetrescue, and regtest with
  -testactivationheight=p2mr_rescue_end@N, above the p2mr_rescue height.
- The mempool follows it, or the next block template would fail
  TestBlockValidity: MempoolScriptFlags adds the restriction as the next
  block will have it, which agrees with the second check on the tip's
  flags; the block that leaves the next one at the height removes the
  transactions with a rescue record, with their descendants; and so does
  a reorganization that leaves the next block at or above the height,
  with the same test as below the lane's own height.
- gettripwireinfo reports end_height, and the lane as closed for the next
  block and for the mempool from there; the startup log names the
  height.

Rescue spends confirmed below the height stay valid.
feature_p2mr_rescue_killswitch.py covers the mempool at the boundary,
reorganizations across it in both directions and one that ends just
below it.
